From cf23a1bff0334f6057167e98fbb2db2210c5b197 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kernel-wasm <kernel-wasm@localhost>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 21:04:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: run arm64 user space

kwemu's guest CPU is AArch64 (kwemu ABI 2: struct kw_regs holds
x0..x30, sp, pc, nzcv, fpcr, fpsr, tpidr_el0 and v0..v31). Present the
arm64 user ABI instead of riscv64's, mirroring arch/arm64 without compat
and without a vDSO:

- syscalls: x8 = number, x0..x5 = arguments, x0 = return; the generic
  table with arm64's ABIs (renameat rlimit memfd_secret), arm64's mmap
  and personality (PER_LINUX32 is refused); riscv_hwprobe and
  riscv_flush_icache are gone (user space flushes with IC IVAU);
- uapi: arm64's fcntl, signal (SA_RESTORER, MINSIGSTKSZ 5120), mman,
  param, posix_types, statfs, auxvec, ptrace, sigcontext, sve_context,
  ucontext and hwcap headers;
- ELF: EM_AARCH64, AT_PLATFORM "aarch64", AT_HWCAP2/3 = 0,
  AT_MINSIGSTKSZ, uname -m aarch64;
- seccomp: filters see AUDIT_ARCH_AARCH64 and arm64's syscall numbers
  (asm/seccomp.h as arm64's without compat, so the cache of syscalls a
  filter always allows spares them the BPF interpreter);
- clone: child x0 = 0, CLONE_SETTLS sets TPIDR_EL0 (CLONE_BACKWARDS as
  before); exec zeroes every register including TPIDR_EL0 and FPCR;
- signals: arm64's rt_sigframe with an fpsimd_context record and a
  frame record; handlers return to sa_restorer when SA_RESTORER is set,
  else to the [sigpage] trampoline (mov x8, #139; svc #0);
- exclusives: every entry to user mode clears the exclusive monitor
  (user_mode_loop(), before each kw_run()), as ERET does on arm64 and
  qemu-user does too, so an STXR after a syscall, a fault, an interrupt
  or a task switch fails and a LDXR/STXR loop retries; that is the only
  place that clears it;
- features: AT_HWCAP is FP | ASIMD | CPUID | the KW_ISA_* features
  kw_set_isa() enables: AES, PMULL, SHA1, SHA2, CRC32, ATOMICS (LSE),
  SHA3 and SHA512 (SHA512 only together with SHA2). kwemu answers the
  MRS of the ID registers itself (ID_AA64ISAR0_EL1 from the enabled
  features), so the kernel emulates none. /proc/cpuinfo is in arm64's
  format for kwemu's MIDR_EL1 (0x000f0000), with the Features line in
  HWCAP bit order. kw.isa= takes armv8, -feat and +feat, with the
  Features names (and "lse" for atomics).

kw_emu.h also states what kwemu's decoded-code cache asks of the kernel:
the flushes it already does (a page that maps rewritten code,
kw_flush_tlb_all(), kw_set_pgd()) make kwemu check decoded blocks again;
*irq_pending is polled at every block end, at least every 64
instructions; IC IVAU on one CPU reaches the others through kwemu's own
code epoch.

The page tables keep the Sv39 format, and TASK_SIZE is 1 << 39, as on
arm64 with 39-bit VAs: the whole range the three levels index, with no
sign-extended upper half (kwemu faults addresses at or above it).
